cases.old:
To check whether the converted muscles pulled as strongly as their OpenSim counterparts, I compared their forces at 121 poses from a reference walk using the same joint positions and muscle activations. The reference forces and activations came from OpenSim static optimization, which estimates how muscles share the load at each pose \citep{opensim_static_optimization_docs}. For eight of twelve right-side muscles, MuJoCo reproduced the variation in force across poses and the mean force agreed within \qty{10}{\percent}. Two muscles illustrate why both checks matter. For the rectus femoris muscle, the mean forces were close, about \qty{48}{
ewton} in OpenSim and \qty{52}{
ewton} in MuJoCo. At one pose, however, OpenSim estimated \qty{96}{
ewton} and MuJoCo estimated \qty{416}{
ewton}. For the short head of the biceps femoris muscle, the forces rose and fell in a similar pattern, but MuJoCo's mean was about \qty{85}{\percent} higher. For both muscles, using the mean OpenSim force at every pose gave a smaller squared error than using the converted model's forces. These two muscles therefore require a separate check before using their forces to interpret a controller test. Appendix~ef{sec:muscle-models-compare} gives the calculation and all twelve results.

cases.revised:
How closely did the converted muscles reproduce the forces estimated in OpenSim? I took 121 poses from a reference walk and applied the OpenSim joint angles and estimated muscle activations to the MuJoCo model, one pose at a time \citep{opensim_static_optimization_docs}. At each pose, MuJoCo used the routed length and prescribed activation to calculate active and passive tension with its rigid-tendon muscle model. Eight of twelve right-side muscles followed the OpenSim force pattern with mean forces within \qty{10}{\percent}. For rectus femoris, similar means (\qty{48}{
ewton} in OpenSim and \qty{52}{
ewton} in MuJoCo) concealed a large error at one pose (\qty{96}{
ewton} versus \qty{416}{
ewton}). For the short head of biceps femoris, the forces rose and fell together, but the MuJoCo mean was about \qty{85}{\percent} higher. For these two muscles a constant equal to the OpenSim mean gave a smaller squared error than the MuJoCo estimates. The reference used a subject-scaled OpenSim model, whereas the conversion began with the generic model. Muscle dimensions, routing, and the force calculations therefore all affect this comparison. No neural controller supplied the activations. Appendix~ef{sec:muscle-models} describes the force equations and Appendix~ef{sec:muscle-models-compare} gives the results for each muscle.
